{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Behold, to YHWH your Elohim belong the heavens, etc.; only in your fathers did YHWH delight.</b> By the word 'רַק' (only) He comes to diminish the heavens and the highest heavens — in all of them YHWH did not delight, but only in your fathers. This accords with the view of the Sages of truth who said: the righteous are greater than the work of the heavens and the earth, and they agreed to say that all of them were created for the sake of man — not according to the view of the Rambam, who wrote that man is primary among the lower beings but has no standing compared with the higher ones. And according to the Rambam's reasoning there is room to explain this verse such that the entire passage flows toward what He said: ״וַאֲהַבְתֶּם אֶת הַגֵּר״ etc., for YHWH desires that you cleave to the converts even though they are lowly; nevertheless, from Him, blessed be He, you shall see and so shall you do, for ״הֵן לַה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ הַשָּׁמַיִם״ etc., and despite the greatness of His exaltedness, blessed be He, nevertheless ״רַק בַּאֲבוֹתֶיךָ חָשַׁק ה׳״ even though they are lowly in comparison to the supernal beings who are near to YHWH. And He gave yet a second sign: ״כִּי ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם הוּא אֱלֹהֵי הָאֱלֹהִים״ etc., and even so, ״עֹשֶׂה מִשְׁפַּט יָתוֹם וְאַלְמָנָה וְאֹהֵב גֵּר״. And from this verse Rabbi Yohanan said: everywhere you find the greatness of the Holy One, blessed be He, there you find His humility, etc. (see Megillah 31a). And from these two premises He brought forth the conclusion that He stated: ״וַאֲהַבְתֶּם אֶת הַגֵּר״." "But one must still examine precisely what He said: ״רַק בַּאֲבוֹתֶיךָ חָשַׁק ה׳ וַיִּבְחַר בְּזַרְעָם אַחֲרֵיהֶם״ — why did He make two divisions? And furthermore, 'delight' (חֵשֶׁק) is not a matter of choice (בְּחִירָה), as is explained above at the end of parashat Va'ethannan (Deuteronomy 7:7). Hear from this that the word 'רַק' (only) refers to what follows it, and comes to say that only in your fathers alone did YHWH delight — with delight and with complete love — but not in you; rather, He chose their seed from all the peoples, meaning that in comparison to the peoples you appear good, for the language of choice applies even to something that is not good in itself, except that it is good relative to its alternatives. Therefore it says ״וַיִּבְחַר בְּזַרְעָם אַחֲרֵיהֶם בָּכֶם״ — for this deficiency depends on you; therefore ״וּמַלְתֶּם אֵת עָרְלַת לְבַבְכֶם״ etc., in order that you may arrive at the level of your fathers."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The Talmud citation (מגילה ל\"א) resolves the abbreviation כו׳ as continuing the known baraita; the page reference follows the standard printed folio."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:00.882468772Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}